8(O), J8(O), K8(X) — no.  \n\nRow 7: H7(O), I7(X) — no.  \n\nNo immediate five exists yet. So instead of a winning move, I must **block Black’s threat and build my own**.  \n\nBlack’s biggest threat:  \n- Open three at H4-H5-H7 (vertical, missing H6 but H6 is O, so not open). Actually H4 empty, H5 X, H6 O, H7 X — no, H7 is O, so not X’s three.  \n\nActually, Black’s potential four: Check I7-J7 — no.  \n\nBetter to strengthen White’s strong vertical line in H by playing **H4** to connect H4-H11 almost, but H10 is X blocking.  \n\nBut there’s a strong diagonal for White:  \nFrom G6 to K10: G6 empty, H7 O, I8 O, J9 O, K10 empty — that’s four O’s diagonal (H7, I8, J9) — wait, that’s only three O’s. Let’s recount: H7(O), I8(O), J9(O) — three in a row diagonal down-right.  \n\nBut if I play **G6**, then G6(O), H7(O), I8(O), J9(O) — four in a row diagonal, and both ends open. That’s a very strong move, creating an open four next turn if not blocked.  \n\nAlso, **K10** would also make four diagonal if played after G6.  \n\nBut immediate win? No.  \n\nBest move is **G6** because:  \n1. Creates diagonal four (G6, H7, I8, J9) — actually only three O’s before move, so after G6 it’s four O’s diagonal.  \n2. Threatens five at F5 or L10 next turn, which is hard to block both ends.  \n3. Also supports vertical H line.  \n\nBut check occupied list: G6 not occupied, so valid.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThis move builds an open four diagonal (after G6, sequence G6-H7-I8-J9 all O’s), which is an unstoppable threat because Black can only block one end, leaving the other end to win next turn.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"col": "G", "row": 6}'}
